{"name":"Pavilion Beige","hex":"#c5b6a4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pavilion","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An ornate tent.","A light roofed structure used as a shelter in a public place."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To furnish with a pavilion.","To put inside a pavilion."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pavilion"},{"term":"beige","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour, variously defined from a pale brown, to a yellow greyish off-white.","Debeige; a kind of woollen or mixed dress goods."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a slightly yellowish gray colour, as that of unbleached wool.","Comfortably dull and unadventurous, in a way that suggests middle-class suburbia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beige"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pavilion","blocks":[{"text":"In architecture, pavilion has several meanings:"},{"items":["It may be a subsidiary building that is either positioned separately or as an attachment to a main building. Often it is associated with pleasure. In palaces and traditional mansions of Asia, there may be pavilions that are either freestanding or connected by covered walkways, as in the Forbidden City, Topkapi Palace in Istanbul, and in Mughal buildings like the Red Fort.","As part of a large palace, pavilions may be symmetrically placed building blocks that flank a main building block or the outer ends of wings extending from both sides of a central building block, the corps de logis. Such configurations provide an emphatic visual termination to the composition of a large building, akin to bookends."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/White_Sawan_or_Bhadon_pavilion.jpg/330px-White_Sawan_or_Bhadon_pavilion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pavilion","wikidata":"Q276173"},{"title":"Beige","blocks":[{"text":"Beige is variously described as a pale sandy fawn color, a grayish tan, a light-grayish yellowish brown, or a pale to grayish yellow. It takes its name from French, where the word originally meant natural wool that has been neither bleached nor dyed, hence also the color of natural wool."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg/330px-Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beige","wikidata":"Q843607"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Casino_marino.JPG/500px-Casino_marino.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Casino_marino.JPG/1280px-Casino_marino.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Casino_marino.JPG/500px-Casino_marino.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Casino at Marino","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACasino_marino.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Louvre_Colonnade_Illustration.png/500px-Louvre_Colonnade_Illustration.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Louvre_Colonnade_Illustration.png/1280px-Louvre_Colonnade_Illustration.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Louvre_Colonnade_Illustration.png/500px-Louvre_Colonnade_Illustration.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration of the classical Louvre Colonnade in Paris with its pavilion highlighted in yellow","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALouvre_Colonnade_Illustration